-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Iso-surface BVH Rebuild:** The Iso-surface BVH must be rebuilt whenever the iso-value changes, as the geometry changes [S1].
-*   **Coordinate Spaces:** Care must be taken when comparing hit distances from the Mesh BVH (world space) and the Iso-surface BVH (volume space). Distances must be converted to a common space before determining the minimum [S1].
-*   **Empty Space Skipping:** Iso-surfaces generally have more potential for empty space skipping due to sparsity compared to dense volume data, justifying separate BVHs [S1].
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Iso-surface BVH Rebuild:** The Iso-surface BVH must be rebuilt whenever the iso-value changes. Failure to do so results in incorrect surface intersections [S1].
+*   **Coordinate Space Transformation:** Rays must be correctly transformed into volume space before querying the Iso-surface BVH. Incorrect transformation leads to missed intersections.
+*   **Precision:** Hardware-accelerated ray-triangle intersection is generally robust, but floating-point precision issues may arise at grazing angles or very small triangles.
+*   **Ambient Occlusion Cost:** Enabling ambient occlusion doubles the ray count for surface hits, significantly increasing computational cost [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by ray-triangle intersection for meshes and ray marching for volumes. Hardware-accelerated intersection for meshes is efficient [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires maintaining separate BVHs for mesh, iso-surface, and volume data. Iso-surface BVH size depends on the complexity of the iso-surface at the given iso-value [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Efficient handling of mixed scene types (surfaces and volumes). Hardware acceleration for meshes reduces overhead. Clipping planes allow flexible control of integration ranges [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires rebuilding the Iso-surface BVH for every iso-value change. Coordinate space conversions add complexity [S1].
-*   **When to use:** Scenes containing both explicit surface geometry (meshes) and volumetric data, where surfaces occlude volume data.
-*   **When not to use:** Scenes with only volumetric data (use pure volume rendering) or only meshes (use pure mesh rendering).
+*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the cost of ray traversal in the BVHs and volume integration. The surface intersection is $O(\log N)$ for BVH traversal, where $N$ is the number of primitives. Volume integration is proportional to the number of samples within $[t_{min}, t_{end}]$.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing three BVHs (Mesh, Iso-surface, Volume). The Iso-surface BVH size depends on the complexity of the isosurface.
+*   **Performance:** Hardware-accelerated ray-triangle intersection (RTX) provides significant speedup for mesh rendering. Empty space skipping via BVHs reduces the number of volume samples.
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient handling of mixed geometry and volume data; high-quality surface rendering; flexible clipping.
+*   **Limitations:** Requires rebuilding Iso-surface BVH for iso-value changes; increased complexity in implementation; potential for aliasing if surface and volume boundaries are not aligned.
 
 ## Background
-This method is part of the ExaBricks rendering framework, which supports various rendering modes including implicit isosurfaces and direct volume rendering [S1]. It was developed to evaluate data structures under realistic conditions involving mixed geometry types [S1].
+This method is part of the ExaBricks rendering framework, which focuses on ray tracing structured AMR (Adaptive Mesh Refinement) data. It was introduced to support realistic evaluation of data structures under conditions involving implicit isosurfaces and direct volume rendering. The approach leverages modern GPU hardware (RTX) for surface intersection, combining it with software-based volume ray marching. It is used in scientific visualization for large-scale datasets, such as those from the Exajet and LANL Impact simulations.
 
